30,2,1,talk,May,,"It's clear the current spot is a nightmare for one neighbor. Also, not all our frontages are equal; some are far too narrow for a bin.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
31,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"I've learned that enclosed structures can actually trap smells and become worse in summer. Also, supervising mornings takes about fifty minutes per shift.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
32,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"We're spending thirty-four thousand yen a year just on maintenance! And a new shelter could cost over two hundred thousand, even with a subsidy.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
33,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I'm worried. Other blocks fought over who cleans the shelter, and some of us aren't comfortable policing our neighbors' trash times.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,1,talk,Servas,,"The clock is a harsh master. A strict window starts very early, and the paperwork for permanent changes or rotations is quite tedious.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,2,talk,May,,"@Mizuki, if the enclosure smells and the supervision is a chore, maybe rotating the location is the only fair way?","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@May, rotating sounds fair, but @Servas mentioned the filing requirements are tedious. We need to weigh the paperwork against the smell.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""May"",""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"But rotating doesn't fix the cost of the nets and deodorants. If we just move the mess, we still pay the maintenance fee.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Kenji, but a permanent structure seems to lead to more arguments about cleaning. I'd rather we avoid long-term conflict.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Servas,,"Indeed. We must consider not just the money, but the time and the spirit of the neighborhood.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,3,talk,May,,"@Kenji, the maintenance cost is low compared to a shelter. Rotating the location means no one is stuck with the smell forever.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"If we rotate, we avoid the ""summer smell"" of a bin house and the stress of policing each other's wake-up times.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"I see. So a shelter is expensive and potentially smelly, and supervision is awkward. Rotating seems like the middle ground.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,3,talk,Midori,,"As long as the rotation is clear and agreed upon, it prevents one person from feeling targeted. It feels the most harmonious.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Servas,,"It is a cycle, like the seasons. Though the paperwork is a burden, it is a one-time effort for a fairer peace.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,4,talk,May,,I'm convinced. Rotating is the only way to ensure no one is unfairly burdened by the location.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"Logically, it avoids the worst pitfalls of the other two options. I'm on board with rotation.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm sold! Let's go with the rotation. It's cheaper than a building and fairer than the current spot.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,4,talk,Midori,,I agree. It keeps us moving and prevents any one person from feeling resentful.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,4,talk,Servas,,I concur. Let us choose the path that distributes the burden and preserves our friendship.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
